  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
